WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.889
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:05.412
Building Ticket-to-Machine.

3
00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:08.687
This is Chapter 1: the
loop and the typed task.

4
00:00:08.687 --> 00:00:11.972
Every step of the workbook's
Ticket-to-Machine loop

5
00:00:11.972 --> 00:00:15.100
writes or reads one record,
the identity finding.

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.125
If its shape is wrong, the clock, the owner
and the re-read are all wrong with it.

7
00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:24.254
We'll place each file of the ServiceNow
and SailPoint kit in the loop,

8
00:00:24.254 --> 00:00:28.604
then check that record on a laptop
before anything reaches ServiceNow,

9
00:00:28.604 --> 00:00:31.037
and finish with two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:31.037 --> 00:00:34.790
The code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:39.890
Why start with the record?

12
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.477
Because the loop has one.

13
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:46.050
A drift becomes a typed task with a
class, a severity, an owner, a clock,

14
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.544
the system where the re-read happens,

15
00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:52.452
and an intended state that says
what the re-read must find.

16
00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:57.416
Get the class wrong and the finding
bounces, so the drift is never recorded.

17
00:00:57.416 --> 00:01:01.328
Choose severity by hand and the
windows stop meaning anything.

18
00:01:01.328 --> 00:01:05.122
And an intended state that
names nothing is the quiet one:

19
00:01:05.122 --> 00:01:09.610
a re-read finds no difference,
and the record closes on nothing.

20
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.091
The workbook's Ticket-to-Machine loop
has five steps: test, detect drift,

21
00:01:18.091 --> 00:01:22.985
raise a typed task, actuate on the
native surface, and close with evidence.

22
00:01:22.985 --> 00:01:24.622
Here's where the kit sits in it.

23
00:01:24.622 --> 00:01:27.211
The drift client tests by joining HR,

24
00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:30.750
Microsoft Graph and
SailPoint on the employee ID.

25
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.684
SailPoint's event triggers detect drift.

26
00:01:33.684 --> 00:01:38.535
The workflows and a ServiceNow scripted
REST resource raise the typed task.

27
00:01:38.535 --> 00:01:40.934
Nothing in the kit actuates yet:

28
00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:45.272
no code writes to Entra ID,
Active Directory or SailPoint.

29
00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:50.070
And a business rule and the re-read
client close only with evidence.

30
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.495
The record those steps share has a short
data contract, kept in the kit's read me.

31
00:01:58.495 --> 00:02:02.439
Every payload says what kind it
is, a finding or a closure signal,

32
00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:07.370
where it came from, and which of the
workbook's seven drift classes it belongs to.

33
00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:12.524
It carries a severity, P1 to P4, the
system where the re-read will happen,

34
00:02:12.524 --> 00:02:17.407
one subject identifier, and the
intended state a re-read must find.

35
00:02:17.407 --> 00:02:20.866
This chapter doesn't restate
where each field lands in the

36
00:02:20.866 --> 00:02:24.757
workbook's evidence record; the
implementation example has that.

37
00:02:24.757 --> 00:02:26.880
It makes the fields checkable.

38
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:31.780
What exists today?

39
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.770
The contract is enforced
in exactly one place,

40
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.468
the scripted REST resource
that runs inside ServiceNow.

41
00:02:38.468 --> 00:02:42.356
It validates the payload and
answers four hundred with a reason.

42
00:02:42.356 --> 00:02:48.206
It maps severity to a priority and
a due date on the workbook's tempo:

43
00:02:48.206 --> 00:02:52.142
P1 immediate, P2 one hour,
P3 a day, P4 three days.

44
00:02:52.142 --> 00:02:55.692
That tempo is the program's
design, not a federal rule.

45
00:02:55.692 --> 00:03:00.102
It defaults severity by class,
de-duplicates against an open finding,

46
00:03:00.102 --> 00:03:03.505
and fills in an intended
state from a SailPoint event.

47
00:03:03.505 --> 00:03:07.820
The catch: nothing outside
ServiceNow applied the same rules.

48
00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.695
So the chapter adds a Python
mirror of the contract.

49
00:03:14.695 --> 00:03:19.574
Its validate function applies the script's
rules and returns the errors ServiceNow

50
00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:23.478
would reject, warnings for what it
would accept but the loop can't use,

51
00:03:23.478 --> 00:03:28.357
and the normalized values, including the
de-duplication key and the response window.

52
00:03:28.357 --> 00:03:32.713
Its constants copy the script's,
and a test reads the JavaScript and

53
00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:36.832
fails if the two drift apart, so
the mirror can't quietly go stale.

54
00:03:36.832 --> 00:03:41.840
A command line checks a file of payloads,
or everything a drift run would post.

55
00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.415
Here's the subtle case the
chapter guards against.

56
00:03:48.415 --> 00:03:51.143
An intended state like
the one on the slide

57
00:03:51.143 --> 00:03:53.715
is a non-empty object,
so it looks present.

58
00:03:53.715 --> 00:03:57.615
But it names nothing: no
attribute, no entitlement.

59
00:03:57.615 --> 00:04:00.519
A re-read compares against
it, finds no difference,

60
00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:02.715
and would close the record on nothing.

61
00:04:02.715 --> 00:04:06.803
The check warns rather than rejects,
because ServiceNow would accept it.

62
00:04:06.803 --> 00:04:11.519
For the two classes the loop sends to
a person first, missing identity and an

63
00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:16.900
unauthorized write, the warning says
that's expected until that person decides.

64
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:22.800
The chapter's tests number twenty-two.

65
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.874
They compare the constants against the
script, try every rejection the script makes,

66
00:04:27.874 --> 00:04:30.483
cover closure signals and system defaults,

67
00:04:30.483 --> 00:04:34.179
check both checkable and empty
intended states and the tempo,

68
00:04:34.179 --> 00:04:38.238
and accept every payload the drift
client builds from the fixtures.

69
00:04:38.238 --> 00:04:41.974
With the rest of the kit, a hundred
and seven Python tests pass,

70
00:04:41.974 --> 00:04:45.337
plus fifteen Node tests of
the ServiceNow scripts and

71
00:04:45.337 --> 00:04:49.220
forty PowerShell tests, all
without a tenant or a network.

72
00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.527
These are the vendor facts
the chapter's code relies on,

73
00:04:56.527 --> 00:04:58.345
each read on November first.

74
00:04:58.345 --> 00:05:02.120
A scripted REST API has a fixed URL shape.

75
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.946
Access is set by API access
policies and endpoint ACLs,

76
00:05:05.946 --> 00:05:11.132
and creating one needs the web service
admin role, which calling it doesn't.

77
00:05:11.132 --> 00:05:15.070
The kit uses two SailPoint
triggers, both fire-and-forget.

78
00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.720
ServiceNow's Government
Community Cloud is FedRAMP High;

79
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.370
SailPoint's Identity Security
Cloud is FedRAMP Moderate.

80
00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:26.770
And a free developer instance is
reclaimed after ten days unused.

81
00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.345
Two points stay open, and the
chapter says so rather than guessing.

82
00:05:34.345 --> 00:05:39.339
First, nobody has confirmed which identity
ServiceNow reports for a call made with

83
00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:42.886
an OAuth client-credentials
token, and the business rule in

84
00:05:42.886 --> 00:05:46.433
Chapter 5 compares exactly that
with its verifier account.

85
00:05:46.433 --> 00:05:51.510
Second, ServiceNow's page says a developer
instance is reclaimed after ten days

86
00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.433
unused, but newer community posts describe
a rule that also considers its age.

87
00:05:56.433 --> 00:05:59.368
So check before relying on
one for more than a few days,

88
00:05:59.368 --> 00:06:01.730
and keep your work in source control.

89
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.493
What does this chapter's output prove?

90
00:06:07.493 --> 00:06:11.263
Every drift becomes one record
with a class, a severity,

91
00:06:11.263 --> 00:06:15.705
an owner and a due date, and a
malformed one is rejected at the door.

92
00:06:15.705 --> 00:06:21.766
That supports configuration change control
and continuous monitoring, CM-3 and CA-7,

93
00:06:21.766 --> 00:06:23.931
both in the Moderate baseline,

94
00:06:23.931 --> 00:06:28.693
and the evidence is the ServiceNow
record plus the contract tests.

95
00:06:28.693 --> 00:06:33.330
And the response window is applied by
code, not chosen ticket by ticket.

96
00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:38.530
A typed finding is only the owned, clocked
half of the workbook's evidence record.

97
00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.250
Approval and the re-read come later.

98
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.062
Who is this for?

99
00:06:46.062 --> 00:06:49.058
Developers who build either
side of the integration,

100
00:06:49.058 --> 00:06:51.576
and the reviewers who
approve their changes.

101
00:06:51.576 --> 00:06:56.575
You need to read Python and JSON, and
you don't need any instance until Lab 2.

102
00:06:56.575 --> 00:07:00.390
One least-privilege point carries
through the whole workbook:

103
00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:05.085
the person who creates the scripted REST
API needs the web service admin role,

104
00:07:05.085 --> 00:07:07.800
but the integration user
who calls it doesn't.

105
00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.190
It gets one custom role, granted through
an endpoint ACL, and nothing else.

106
00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.690
Lab 1 needs only a laptop with Python.

107
00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:22.640
First, run the chapter's twenty-two tests.

108
00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.328
Then check five sample payloads at a
fixed clock, noon on November first.

109
00:07:27.328 --> 00:07:30.205
The first is accepted: it had no severity,

110
00:07:30.205 --> 00:07:34.390
so the class default gave it P2
and a due time one hour later.

111
00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:39.153
The second has a typo in its class and
is rejected with the list of valid ones.

112
00:07:39.153 --> 00:07:43.615
The third is accepted but warned:
its intended state names nothing.

113
00:07:43.615 --> 00:07:45.435
The fourth has a bad severity,

114
00:07:45.435 --> 00:07:49.590
and the fifth is a closure signal
that never closes anything.

115
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.365
Next, run the ServiceNow script's
own fifteen tests in Node,

116
00:07:57.365 --> 00:07:59.377
the rules the Python mirrors.

117
00:07:59.377 --> 00:08:01.940
Then run the drift reconciliation over the

118
00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.716
fixtures and check every
payload it would post.

119
00:08:04.716 --> 00:08:06.102
Twelve are accepted.

120
00:08:06.102 --> 00:08:10.857
Three carry the warning, and they're
exactly the classes a person decides first:

121
00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:14.002
two missing identities and
the unauthorized write.

122
00:08:14.002 --> 00:08:19.390
Notice Erin's two attribute drifts
in Entra ID, department and manager.

123
00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.053
They now have separate keys.

124
00:08:21.053 --> 00:08:23.452
Before Chapter 4's fix, they shared one,

125
00:08:23.452 --> 00:08:27.000
and the second would have
vanished into the first.

126
00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.667
Lab 2 moves to your own ServiceNow
Personal Developer Instance,

127
00:08:34.667 --> 00:08:37.550
never production, and the
authors haven't run it.

128
00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.672
You build the finding table and the
scripted REST resource from the kit,

129
00:08:41.672 --> 00:08:46.213
lock it down with authentication and an
endpoint ACL for one integration user,

130
00:08:46.213 --> 00:08:48.937
then check a payload
locally before posting it.

131
00:08:48.937 --> 00:08:52.913
Expect a created record with
priority 2, due an hour later.

132
00:08:52.913 --> 00:08:55.350
Post it again and it de-duplicates.

133
00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:59.525
Post the typo and you get the same
four hundred Lab 1 predicted.

134
00:08:59.525 --> 00:09:01.100
Then back it all out.

135
00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.300
Each danger has a guard.

136
00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.317
If the Python mirror drifts from
the script, a payload passes on a

137
00:09:10.317 --> 00:09:14.638
laptop and fails in ServiceNow, so
a test reads the JavaScript itself.

138
00:09:14.638 --> 00:09:19.576
A typo in a class would lose the drift
entirely, so it fails locally first.

139
00:09:19.576 --> 00:09:22.803
An empty intended state would
close a record on nothing,

140
00:09:22.803 --> 00:09:24.838
so it's warned now and skipped later.

141
00:09:24.838 --> 00:09:28.902
Severity chosen per ticket would
empty the windows of meaning,

142
00:09:28.902 --> 00:09:30.700
so defaults live in one table.

143
00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:35.510
And a developer instance isn't permanent:
keep your work in source control.

144
00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:40.610
Here's what done looks like.

145
00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:44.399
The Python constants match the
script that's actually deployed,

146
00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:45.972
and the parity test passes.

147
00:09:45.972 --> 00:09:48.470
Every client that posts findings runs its

148
00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.323
payloads through the
validator in its own tests.

149
00:09:51.323 --> 00:09:55.723
Nothing a client builds is rejected,
and every warning is understood.

150
00:09:55.723 --> 00:09:58.642
The windows are the program's
current ones and the

151
00:09:58.642 --> 00:10:01.847
severity defaults match the
workbook's drift classes.

152
00:10:01.847 --> 00:10:05.535
And the API requires
authentication and a custom role.

153
00:10:05.535 --> 00:10:11.048
Next, Chapter 2 writes down every ServiceNow
call and the least privilege it needs.

154
00:10:11.048 --> 00:10:12.210
Thanks for watching.
